 is the object of the transitive verb.","Choose **the evening meal**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This transitive clause names the thing involved in the action.","B":"**the evening meal** answers what the assistant prepares; it is the object of the transitive verb.","C":"This is the subject performing the action.","D":"This is the verb, not its object."}}},{"id":"8ef20bec-2581-5cf7-a713-dbc38fb95d73","topicSlug":"sentence-structure","level":"A1","prompt":"Complete the main clause after a reason: “Because there is time, ____ the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"our"},{"id":"B","text":"we prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"D","text":"prepareing"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Because introduces a dependent reason clause. The rest still needs a **main clause**.","explanation":"**We prepare** supplies the subject and finite verb for the main clause.","keywords":["clause-completion"],"skill":"clause-completion","contextKey":"sentence-structure-49","fingerprint":"16e7f980b8e5a939059cc7b273d09eec560fb1bb8099c77cbbf42f685570a0db","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Because introduces a dependent reason clause. The rest still needs a **main clause**.","**We prepare** supplies the subject and finite verb for the main clause.","Choose **we prepare**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A possessive determiner cannot form the missing clause.","B":"**We prepare** supplies the subject and finite verb for the main clause.","C":"A to-infinitive does not supply a finite main clause here.","D":"This form does not supply a subject and finite verb."}}},{"id":"a75f7393-5a2d-53be-a37c-58f11ffc47a5","topicSlug":"sentence-structure","level":"A1","prompt":"In “The assistant repairs the rowing boat carefully”, which words are the subject?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"the rowing boat"},{"id":"B","text":"carefully"},{"id":"C","text":"The assistant"},{"id":"D","text":"repairs"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Find who or what performs the action, then separate it from the action itself.","explanation":"**The assistant** is the subject: it tells us who performs the action.","keywords":["subject-identification"],"skill":"subject-identification","contextKey":"sentence-structure-50","fingerprint":"42212a8c62d264e7e2cb7f23852c69d5e8fc0a1e23a2014491c1ee18b903393b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Find who or what performs the action, then separate it from the action itself.","**The assistant** is the subject: it tells us who performs the action.","Choose **The assistant**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This is the object involved in the action.","B":"This adverb describes how the action is performed.","C":"**The assistant** is the subject: it tells us who performs the action.","D":"This is the verb, which expresses the action."}}},{"id":"ef0355de-69ff-55bd-a82b-874115b01441","topicSlug":"sentence-structure","level":"A1","prompt":"In “The assistant repairs the rowing boat carefully”, which word is the main verb?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"assistant"},{"id":"B","text":"carefully"},{"id":"C","text":"the rowing boat"},{"id":"D","text":"repairs"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Look for the word that expresses the action.","explanation":"**repairs** expresses the action and agrees with the singular subject.","keywords":["verb-identification"],"skill":"verb-identification","contextKey":"sentence-structure-50","fingerprint":"6ce3958c4a9604f1d758ee772b50a0668a5a907a7cd62c19b8df05729d7ec16d","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Look for the word that expresses the action.","**repairs** expresses the action and agrees with the singular subject.","Choose **repairs**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Assistant is a noun within the subject phrase.","B":"Carefully modifies the verb; it is an adverb.","C":"This noun phrase is the object, not the verb.","D":"**repairs** expresses the action and agrees with the singular subject."}}},{"id":"96f149b7-60cc-50ac-a40c-499280fc0657","topicSlug":"sentence-structure","level":"A1","prompt":"In “The assistant repairs the rowing boat”, which phrase is the object?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"the rowing boat"},{"id":"B","text":"The assistant"},{"id":"C","text":"repairs"},{"id":"D","text":"There is no object"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Ask the verb’s question: the assistant does this to or with **what**?","explanation":"**the rowing boat** answers what the assistant repairs; it is the object of the transitive verb.","keywords":["object-identification"],"skill":"object-identification","contextKey":"sentence-structure-50","fingerprint":"af6412ed2c7914bbe6cbfa2e623c6d741ee0c7e69e0f98e66504c0c0e29ed83e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Ask the verb’s question: the assistant does this to or with **what**?","**the rowing boat** answers what the assistant repairs; it is the object of the transitive verb.","Choose **the rowing boat**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**the rowing boat** answers what the assistant repairs; it is the object of the transitive verb.","B":"This is the subject performing the action.","C":"This is the verb, not its object.","D":"This transitive clause names the thing involved in the action."}}},{"id":"f42692e8-d2e7-51c9-a0bc-f08fad76c35d","topicSlug":"sentence-structure","level":"A1","prompt":"Complete the main clause after a reason: “Because there is time, ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"our"},{"id":"C","text":"we repair"},{"id":"D","text":"to repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Because introduces a dependent reason clause. The rest still needs a **main clause**.","explanation":"**We repair** supplies the subject and finite verb for the main clause.","keywords":["clause-completion"],"skill":"clause-completion","contextKey":"sentence-structure-50","fingerprint":"bddffa39a0d49c93e093e00fe5d07f49578ab41b4f0efc386104d567fb71af53","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["dfe-grammar"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Because introduces a dependent reason clause. The rest still needs a **main clause**.","**We repair** supplies the subject and finite verb for the main clause.","Choose **we repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This form does not supply a subject and finite verb.","B":"A possessive determiner cannot form the missing clause.","C":"**We repair** supplies the subject and finite verb for the main clause.","D":"A to-infinitive does not supply a finite main clause here."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Start with the building blocks: subjects, verbs, objects, complements and complete clauses.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Find the subject and the verb","explanation":"A clause normally contains a **subject** and a **verb phrase**. The subject tells us who or what the clause is about. The verb expresses an action, event or state. In a simple statement, the subject usually comes before the verb. A subject can contain several words.","pattern":"**The new guide** **explains** the route.","commonMistakes":["The first noun you see is not always the subject: in On Monday, the guide arrives, Monday belongs to a time phrase."],"examples":["In “The assistant prepares the visitor guide carefully”, which words are the subject? → **The assistant**","In “The assistant restores the old portrait carefully”, which words are the subject? → **The assistant**"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Objects and complements","explanation":"A transitive verb can take an **object**, the person or thing affected or involved. After a linking verb such as be or seem, a **complement** describes or identifies the subject instead. Compare She paints the door with The door is blue. Blue describes the door; it is not a thing receiving an action.","pattern":"She opens **the door**. / The door is **heavy**.","commonMistakes":["Some verbs need no object: the baby sleeps. Do not force every sentence into subject–verb–object."],"examples":["In “The assistant prepares the visitor guide carefully”, which word is the main verb? → **prepares**","In “The assistant restores the old portrait carefully”, which word is the main verb? → **restores**"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Word class depends on its job","explanation":"A noun names something, an adjective describes a noun, and an adverb can modify a verb, adjective or clause. The same spelling may have different jobs: a clean room uses clean as an adjective; clean the room uses it as a verb. Identify the word in its sentence.","pattern":"They **carefully** open the **heavy** door.","commonMistakes":["An -ly ending is only a clue: friendly is usually an adjective, and fast can be an adjective or adverb."],"examples":["In “The assistant prepares the visitor guide”, which phrase is the object? → **the visitor guide**","In “The assistant restores the old portrait”, which phrase is the object? → **the old portrait**"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"A complete written sentence","explanation":"A written sentence needs a complete main clause and suitable final punctuation. A dependent clause introduced by because or although usually needs a main clause beside it. Commands often leave the subject you unspoken, so Open the door is complete.","pattern":"**Because it was cold, we closed the window.**","commonMistakes":["Because it was cold alone is a fragment in ordinary formal prose, though fragments have purposeful uses in conversation and creative writing."],"examples":["Complete the main clause after a reason: “Because there is time, **we prepare** the visitor guide.”","Complete the main clause after a reason: “Because there is time, **we restore** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**The new guide** **explains** the route.","She opens **the door**. / The door is **heavy**.","They **carefully** open the **heavy** door.","**Because it was cold, we closed the window.**"]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":[]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
